// The value of X is the number of unit steps from x down to 0.  On the
// integers it is x for x >= 0, so the lower bound X 3 >= 3 holds and the
// linear post-fixpoint X x = x (for x >= 0) proves it; on the reals the same
// equation has the value floor(x), and no linear post-fixpoint valid at
// every real state reaches more than x - 1, so a bound checker that relaxes
// the integer state to a real one cannot prove the bound.
X 3 >= 3.0
s.t.
X (x : int) : real =mu if x >= 1 then 1.0 + X (x - 1) else 0.0;
